Andy Glew wrote:
> 
> >  There is row address latching.  Otherwise multibank designs couldn't share
> >common row address bussing and predecoders.
> 
> There is row ADDRESS latching.
> 
> Is there row WORDLINE latching?

  There are designs that use each.

> 
> I.e. is the row address latched at input to the decoder,
> or at output? All of the ISSCC papers I have seen suggest
> the former.  If this is coupled with wordline switching
> - not driving the whole wordline, but only driving the half
> that sits in the subarray you want - then you have
> distinct intra and inter subarray timing.

  I am not sure what you are getting at.  I think it is a
  simple issue of mismatched nomenclature.  An array is
  simply a rectangular group of memory cells bounded on the
  right side (arbitary designation) or right and left side
  by row decoders and word line drivers.  Sense amp strips
  run along the top *and* bottom because of the 2x pitch
  mismatch.  The number of word lines in the array is either
  256 or 512 depending on there being 128 or 256 cells per
  bitline (we are talking folded bitline architecture and
  ignoring redundancy spares).  Often multiple arrays are
  stacked on top of each other and share the sense amps
  between them. This stack itself would form a small section
  of a complete device.  Depending on the architecture (i.e
  # of logical rows and columns per device/bank, this can be
  designed with much flexibility) a single array/wordline
  can be actived or a number of non-adjacent arrays/wordlines
  can be activated in response to a single logical page open.
  Regardless of the big pircture organization the basic array
  size (what I guess you call a sub-array) defines the row
  access timing.

> 
> (And while we are at it, I sure would like it if we had wordline latching
> controlled separately - so that you could be reading out one row
> while performing the row decode for the same subarray.)

  I think you talking about pipelining the various operations
  in DRAM access.  This is generally frowned upon because the
  various sub-operations tend to be self-timed  chains that
  start from major externally visible operations like raising
  a word line.  DRAMs with synchronous interfaces tend to be
  implemented as a shallow digital veneer over essentially the
  classic analog RAS/CAS controlled DRAM core.  DRAMs are more
  difficult to pipeline than SRAMs because they have a lot of
  little things that must be done with an unforgiving degree of
  care.  If you could intimately pipeline it I would guess your
  row and column access times would be longer than would be
  generally acceptable and the operating frequency would have
  a narrow allowable range.


All opinions strictly my own.
--
