One approach that you can use is the ASCII export function of the DECmate II
and capture the data on your PC.  It will only allow you to transfer the
ASCII characters.  You'll lose all of the document formatting
characteristics but at least you can move the text of the document.  You'll
have to recreate all page breaks, fonts, margins, etc. on the PC.

I think this has been discussed in comp.os.vms before.  You can try Deja
News and see what you can find.

If I remember correctly, there is a Document Exchange (DX) function in the
DECmate II.  It allows you to transmit the text of a document to the DECmate
serial port.  You'll need to have a serial cable in a null-modem
configuration connected between the DECmate and your PC.  I think the
DECmate defaults to 9600 baud, 8 bits, no parity on its serial port.

On the PC side, you'll need to use Kermit, Windows Terminal, HyperTerm or
a similar utility to capture the text.  It will probably take quite a bit of
trial and error to get it all to work, but you should be able to do it
eventually.


