Andy Glew wrote:
[snip]
> But, if they are laid out in a strip, with even sense amps on one side and
> odd sense amps on the other, you get this N-1/N+1 restriction.
> Q: what happens and the end of such a "strip" - surely it doesn't wrap?

  The top and bottom arrays only have to worry about one neighbour.

> 
> This N-1/N/N+1 has a wierd performance characteristic. In some ways it is as if
> you have 1/4 the number of larger granularity banks.  Except that, there is a penalty
> for accessing the "nearest neighbours".

   It certainly does.  Here are the results of my simulations of a large
   number of trials opening banks at random for 16 and 32 dependent banks.
   The figures indicate the fraction of trials that got stuck after opening
   the indicated number of banks due to N+/-1 conflict:
              
    Maximum banks    N = 16      N = 32
    that could be
    opened

       1             12.9 %      6.3 %
       2             22.8 %     11.9 %
       3             25.9 %     15.8 %
       4             20.9 %     17.3 %
       5             12.1 %     16.1 %
       6              4.5 %     13.0 %
       7              0.9 %      9.3 %
       8             ~0.0 %      5.6 %
       9                         2.9 %
      10                         1.2 %
      11                         0.4 %
      12                         0.1 %

> 
> Now, by the way, I am fairly sure that I have seen this N-1/N/N+1 structure in
> another manufacturer's standard, non-RAMbus, DRAMs. Isn't it standard?

  Not that I am aware of.  The standard definition of a bank is a section
  of memory that can have a logical row activated independently of the
  state of any other bank.

> Although I suppose that EDO DRAMs give you little or no ability to access
> the bank structure, so that it doesn't matter.
> 
> Paul: would you care to describe the bank/sense amp structure of the DRAMs
> you work on?  And of the largest market share SDRAMs you are familiar with?

  Old style DRAM like FPM and EDO didn't have a multi bank structure.  The
  first SDRAMs had two banks and I think the latest have four.  I designed
  on the command processing and control pipelines of the 72 Mb demonstration
  SLDRAM device and it has eight completely independent banks.

  The problem with banks is they cost area due to duplication of sense
  amp strips (the largest features other than the cell arrays themselves)
  between bank boundaries.  The benefit of numerous banks is far less
  for typical PC applications than a large SMP server so it is hard to
  please everyone.

All opinions strictly my own.
