>> Now, by the way, I am fairly sure that I have seen this N-1/N/N+1 structure in
>> another manufacturer's standard, non-RAMbus, DRAMs. Isn't it standard?
>
>  Not that I am aware of.  The standard definition of a bank is a section
>  of memory that can have a logical row activated independently of the
>  state of any other bank.
>
>> Although I suppose that EDO DRAMs give you little or no ability to access
>> the bank structure, so that it doesn't matter.
>>
>> Paul: would you care to describe the bank/sense amp structure of the DRAMs
>> you work on?  And of the largest market share SDRAMs you are familiar with?
>
>  Old style DRAM like FPM and EDO didn't have a multi bank structure.  The
>  first SDRAMs had two banks and I think the latest have four.

ISSCC 96:

A 32 bank 1 Gb SDRAM with 1 Gb/s bandwidth, Yoo et al, Samsung
describe a DRAM with 32 banks, as in their title: 8 128 Mb arrays, each of which
is built up out of 8 16 Mb arrays, each of which is built up out of 128Kb blocks
aranged in an XY grid.  Vertically neighbouring 128 Kb blocks appear to share
sense amp rows, except when separated by wiring.

A 2.5 ns clock access 250 MHz 256 Mb SDRAM with synchronous mirror delay,
Saeki et al, NEC.
"8" banks, number 0-3L, and 0-4U (lower/upper), with row decoders shared between
banks 0L and 1L, 0U and 1U, etc. I.e. effectively 4 banks.
Sense amp slices appear to be shared between even horizontally adjacent
128Kb sub-arrays.

ISSCC 96 is the only one I have the CD for at home.

It seems to me that the technique of sharing sense amps and/or decoders between
adjacent memory arrays is quite common. Necessarily, then, when one such sub-array
is active, the sub-arrays it shares with are inactive (unless, e.g. there are latches isolating
the row decoders from the word lines --- which I really want to see in DRAMs, so that they
can be pipelined better, but which nobody does in commodity DRAMs.)

Now, one can always take a group of such sub-arrays with shared sense amps or decoders,
and declare it a bank --- and then the banks can be independently accessed.

Furthermore, when the sub-arrays only number 2 in any dimension, or when a sub-array has all of its
sense amps on one side, so that at most two sub-arrays share a strip, then, again, the strictly
hierarchical grouping into totally independent banks is apparent.

However, if your sense amps are between 1 and 2X the pitch of the bitlines,
and when you decide not to limit pitch by placing even sense amps on one side,
odd on the other, and sharing (methinks I have seen DRAMs where, even with this even/odd
split, the SRAMs needed to be staggered to pitch match the DRAM cells, i.e. where the sense
amps where between 2X and 4X the bitline pitch), then the hierarchical grouping
becomes less aparrent. Similarly for row decoders.

In all cases, when sense amps or decoders are shared between sub-arrays, then it should be faster
to access a different address in the active sub-array than it would be to access an adjacent
sub-array.  There must be some time required to switch the sense amps from upper
to lower, or row decoders from left to right (unless you always drive the entire wordline (power!)
or latch as I mention above).  With luck that switching time may be less than the time to decode
a new intra-bank address, but I suspect that oftentimes it is just "rolled into" the intra-bank address
switching delay --- perhaps because people are not ready to think about a 3rd level of inside-the-DRAM
hierarchy: independent banks, sub-arrays within a bank, bank to bank switching time, sub-array to sub-array
switching time, and switching time within a sub-array.

But just because something is worst-cased doesn't mean a better value does not exist.
And that smart people should not try to take advantage of it.
It looks to me like RDRAM is just exposing a 3rd timing parameter that is already
present. Probably motivated by the desire to share as many sense amps as possible,
to make up for the RAMbus area overhead.  A conventional DRAM could be designed
the same way, for a modest area savings with a more complicated timing model.

By the way, how does MDRAM do it?

>  The problem with banks is they cost area due to duplication of sense
>  amp strips (the largest features other than the cell arrays themselves)
>  between bank boundaries.  The benefit of numerous banks is far less
>  for typical PC applications than a large SMP server so it is hard to
>  please everyone.


Well, I want as many banks as possible in standard PC applications
(mainly graphics).  But I don't want to pay a chip count or an area
premium for them. So I'll take the shared sense amp slice tricks,
especially if I can fairly easily permute the address bits so that
the sub-array switching time does not impact spatial locality.

I agree that this doesn't quite match up with the traditional concept of
totally independent banks. Oh, well.






