Read something besides Consumer Reports.

Has anyone ever stopped to wonder whether the frequency of repair data
on which Consumer Reports relies so heavily is actually of any true
relevance?

Think about it: if I have a supply line that is 12,000 miles long, I might
have to design a car that doesn't rely on that supply line very often
during the warranty period.  If my supply line is 500 miles long and
I have a massive dealer maintenance network I might find it reasonable
to replace a timing belt once in awhile under warranty but Honda, at
least early on when foreign car maintenance costs were extremely high,
may have found it necessary to avoid this.

So, certainly, it is important to have a car that does not have to be
in the shop very often.  But I submit that American cars are highly
reliable as well and that Consumer Reports is using frequency of
repair statistics instead of a more balanced evaluation because they
are easy to get by polling their readers.

I'm still driving a Ford Escort that I bought new many years ago
and that Consumer Reports seems to think is a maintenance headache,
and all I've had to do to it that wasn't due to something I caused
was replace a fusible link - plus the usual maintenance items such
as tuneups, filters, and wheel alignments.  And the car has actually
had fewer problems in the last five years, all out of warranty,
than in the first.

