>  As an aside, with the apparent news that rambus will increase
>  the number of "banks" from 16 to 32, I wrote a little C program
>  to simulate the effect of this increase in the face of random
>  bank activations.  As you may or may not know, rambus "banks"
>  share sense amp strips so if you have bank N open you are
>  forbidden to open banks N-1 or N+1.

Surely you mean "if you want to open bank N, you cannot open
bank N^1".

Not "if you want to open bank N, you cannot open bank N-1 and N+1".


Whoops... I just showed my own ignorance. Which I am willing to do,
if I learn as a result.

I had a mental model that the banks were "twinned" - paired, sharing
sense amps between pairs. Efectively halving the number of banks.

But, if they are laid out in a strip, with even sense amps on one side and
odd sense amps on the other, you get this N-1/N+1 restriction.
Q: what happens and the end of such a "strip" - surely it doesn't wrap?

This N-1/N/N+1 has a wierd performance characteristic. In some ways it is as if
you have 1/4 the number of larger granularity banks.  Except that, there is a penalty
for accessing the "nearest neighbours".


Now, by the way, I am fairly sure that I have seen this N-1/N/N+1 structure in
another manufacturer's standard, non-RAMbus, DRAMs. Isn't it standard?
Although I suppose that EDO DRAMs give you little or no ability to access
the bank structure, so that it doesn't matter.


Paul: would you care to describe the bank/sense amp structure of the DRAMs
you work on?  And of the largest market share SDRAMs you are familiar with?





So in the standard 64 Mb
>  DRDRAM with 16 banks you could never open more than 8.  In the
>  face of random sequences of bank activation requests, the 16
>  bank scheme can open an average of 3.1 banks before being unable
>  to open a bank due to the adjacency restriction. If the banks
>  are increased to 32, the average banks open before conflict only
>  increases to 4.6.  This is with purely random bank requests; if
>  there is any spacial locality it would likely only be worse.
