>Paul: would you care to describe the bank/sense amp structure of the DRAMs
>you work on?  And of the largest market share SDRAMs you are familiar with?


By the way, I have a selfish motive here: I am just implementing the RAM
part of the simulator I use at school (SimpleScalar), and I would like to have a fairly
generic structure that can be parameterized for realistic DRAM bank, page,
and other layouts. As opposed to the flat access model that many CPU simulators
use for memory.

If anyone can point me to existing public domain code that models such fairly accurately,
I would appreciate it.  Or even pointing me to a complete overview of the full memory system.

The part that always trips me up is when someone like Paul says "RDRAM disables banks
N-1 and N+1 when accessing bank N", but then some smart memory controller designer
goes and swaps address bits so that bank N-1 and N+1 are actually a set of discontiguous
address ranges not physically adjacent to bank N, but at an offset like i*64K,
in an attempt to meet Paul's objection that the N-1/N/N+1 banking structure should bite even
worse on access patterns with spatial locality.

At least, such address permutation tricks used to be the bread and butter of memory system
performance tuning. Is it possible that they are no longer done?







