Aaron Spink wrote:
[snip]
> On a related not, do you think that there is no need for a
> packetized/high bandwidth per pin memory interface?  What do you see
> as the future memory interface?  Assuming that people are simply not
> going to change the core(proven at least so far for mass market) then
> what do you think should be done?

  In an ideal world the CPU would contain its own DRAM, many tens of
  independent banks, each an entire cache line wide.  The CPU would
  have links like the EV7 for gluelessly wiring these things together.
  If you want more memory you get more processing power and vice versa.
  Maybe you even have CPU less nodes, just memory and routing switches
  to adjust the mix in a non-homogenous mesh.  Obviously we are far
  from this point today but the inevitable pull of integration will
  lead in this direction and I hope someday to participate in it. The
  Mitsubishi M32R/D and Patterson's IRAM are the first steps in this
  direction.

  Until this can be realized maybe 5-10 years down the road (first in
  portable and embedded control applications, and slowly moving up
  the performance/volume pyramid to big iron desktop and server CPUs)
  the DRAM will be at least on separate dice, most likely in separate
  packages.  So how do we deal with this?

  The big problem with rambus IMHO is it that it focuses on bandwidth
  to the detriment of latency.  The direct rambus interface took the
  classic rambus and attempts a brute force scaling up.  It's now a
  grotesque caricature of its former self with 24" transmission lines
  snaking on and off modules and multiple timing zones etc.  And even
  if you can get it to work reliably at 800 Mbps, where do you go from
  there?  How do you get the rambus signaling scheme to work with ever
  less voltage tolerant processes?

  I agree that an interface with very low voltage swing, low impedance
  drivers, incident wave switching, possibly differential signalling,
  to maximize bandwidth per pin makes sense.  But the rambus scheme of
  dribbling address bits to the memory in narrow packets stretched
  to the same length as the data burst seems one-sided to me (call
  me a latency bigot).  I would prefer an interface with two source
  synchronous unidirectional links, one to the memory for commands,
  addresses, and write data, and another from the memories back to the
  controller/CPU for read data.  The downstream path is wide enough so
  that a single command bit (to indicate a page read) plus bank and
  column address can be transmitted in a single beat.  Other commands
  would utilize a second beat for an extended command code and row
  address if needed.  Optimize for the frequent, performance limiting,
  latency critical case first.  The interface would be asymmetrical in
  bandwidth because of the shared command/address/write data bus.  The
  telecom guys would hate it but for computer applications reads far
  outweigh writes and writes can be buffered.  The DRAMs would have
  many fully independent banks built with small arrays employing
  direct bitline sensing and independent read and write data paths to
  maximize performance and minimize access time and read/write turn-
  around time.  This would all come with a price but if you are going
  to build a DRAM for high performance then do it right :)

All opinions strictly my own, obviously.
--
Paul W. DeMone       The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race of EPIC
Kanata, Ontario      proportions to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com    architectures with MIPSed results but ALPHA's well
pdemone@igs.net      that ends well.



