Dana Holland wrote:
> 
> "Martin P." wrote:
> >
> > As support and knowledge goes down in Austria and prices are still high we
> > replace some alphas with NT...
> > New NT-Systems are cheaper than some y2k upgrades of VMS.
> 
> These are the kinds of things that I've been hearing recently.  We are
> at a crossroads with our administrative system here, and trying to
> decide whether to stay with our current product which can run on a
> multitude of platforms (we're currently AIX, but could easily switch to
> NT); or we could switch to another product that *only* runs on VMS.
> Obviously, I'm a little leery of the latter option, considering what
> I've been hearing; I've also been told that the Gartner Group has
> documented the steady decline of VMS.

Might I suggest you look at the numbers for yourself. The Q has 'em and
most likely will share 'em. As I have reported in Shannon Knows Compaq,
the alleged steady decline has yet to materialize. There are still over
400K OVMS licenses out there, down somewhat from 434K a couple of years
ago, but significantly higher than the Key Influencers predicted
(Probability Factor: .95).

VMS remains an extremely high-margin business, and one that generates
significant revenue. Based on guidance from the Q, I'd put the aggregate
VMS biz at $4B per annum.

