  that BMWs sell poorly to the lowest income quintile of the
  population too. Beginning of the end?  - something has to
  exist first before it can end.

  Any potential opportunity in the $2000 and under market has
  been closed since the 21164PC and its descendent fell off
  the integer performance curve years ago.  The only mass market
  FP applications at this end of the spectrum are 3D games and
  x86 has a mortal lock on customer base and ISVs.
  
  DEQ has realized this and hasn't tried to market to this end.
  The hardware manual for the shrunk 21164PC has been on the
  DEC Alpha OEM web site since December but the part hasn't
  even been announced yet alone appeared in anyone's plans AFAIK.

  Even if the 21264 price is driven down, its integer advantage
  over x86 is minimal, especially after you take into account
  the x86 software performance biases built into the M$ world.
  Instead DEQ should work hard catering to the Linux market
  by bringing Tru64 compiler technology to Linux and making
  bare bones, powerful EV6 boxes widely available.  Below $2000
  is a meat grinder; but solutions in the $3000 - $5000 range
  might be interesting to professionals and FP enthusiasts.

All opinions strictly my own.
