Henk Borsje wrote:
> 
> One of my RZ25's is reporting frequent errors and probalby will need
> replacement.  I still use VMS 5.5 on the machine.  What is a good
> third-party disk that can serve as replacement?
> 
> I know there is a maximum size of 1 GIG drives on VAXstations 3100. Is
> the limit on a 4000/60 different?
> 
> After replacing the disk (it is not the system disk), is an 'init'
> command sufficient to set up the disk?


The VAXstation 4000-60s don't have the 1GB limit.

VMS 5.5 has a 8GB limit for each volume.  If you want to use a disk
larger than 8GB, you need to partition it into smaller volumes.  If you
want, the volumes may then be bound together as a bound volume set to
give you back your original disk size as one logical volume.

Instead of doing an INIT on the disk, I would hook it up at the same
time as the RZ25 and do a BACKUP/IMAGE from the RZ25 to the new disk. 
Then remove the RZ25, reset SCSI IDs as necessary, and go with it.

 As others have mentioned, VMS 5.5 has problems with some of the newer
SCSI drives.  There are some SCSI patches for 5.5-2 which help some, but
I don't know if they also apply to 5.5.  Also, it may be necessary to
fiddle with the ARRE and ARWE bits on drives which have been configured
for the PC market.

Having said all of that, I am using a RZ28 (DEC 3/12" 2GB, internal) and
Seagate 3GB and 9GB drives (both 5 1/4" in an external enclosure) on my
VS4000-60.  All work well.

Good luck!

