Tim Cullip writes...

> We are in the process of setting up 11 XP1000 machines under
> OSF 4.0e (these were purchased as upgrades when we purchased
> DPW 500au's last year) and have noticed that we don't get any
> sound coming out from the sound chip.
>
> You can run things like /usr/bin/mme/audiocontrol (which gives
> you the widget set to set audio controls) and also
> /usr/bin/X11/demos/xcd to play a CD (and tell that it is playing
> because audiocontrol's VU meter shows sound output levels, and you
> can hear the CD when you use headphones), but no sound comes out
> of the internal speaker when the CD is playing.

    The internal speaker on the Compaq Professional
    Workstation XP1000 (code name: Monet) is hooked up only
    to the PC beeps generated by the keyboard controller in
    the southbridge .  With Tru64 UNIX, I set the frequency
    and duration with the "xset b [[[<volume in percentage>]
    <frequency in Hz>] <duration in ms>]" command;  the volume
    setting doesn't work (except to set it off) with this
    command because the volume control is in the audio chip,
    not the southbridge.  I set the volume of the beep using
    the "sysconfig -r mmsess Pc_Speaker=<volume>" command,
    where <volume ranges from 0 (off) to 7 (maximum).

    The regular audio stream is sent to the front headphone
    jack and to the rear external speaker jack, but not to
    the internal speaker.  Plugging in headphones disables
    audio to the rear speaker jack.

> By the way, the XP1000 is an amazingly fast workstation (2.5x the
> memory bandwidth of the old 500au)!

    Glad you like it, and thanks for the words of praise.

    -- Kenny

