<rhealy@whoi.edu> wrote:


>I'm running on an Alpha using F77 and I'm getting an underflow error
>that causes my program to halt.   Now, according to the f77 man page if
>I specify -fpe4 option the program will "continue [...] execution if a
>floating-point operation results in overflow, a division by zero,
>invalid data, or floating-point underflow...."  However, this does not
>prevent the program from stopping.  My other (relevant) options are 
>-noerror_limit -fast. The error is occurring in the DEXP function for
>which a return value of 0 will suffice for the underflow.  It is not
>practical to code in an error check myself as this is a large climate
>model and the error is bound to occur elsewhere.  Any help appreciated. 

You don't say what compiler or OS version you are using - but I have
to wonder why you're using -fpe4 and not the default -fpe0 if you want
underflows to go to zero (or perhaps -fpe2).  If you want speed (and
your use of -fast suggests this), then -fpe0 is preferable.

I tried a short example with a current version of the compiler (V5.2)
and didn't see any errors with an underflow from DEXP.




