Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec

There has been a lot of news lately about the problems and delays
Intel is encountering while trying to transition PCs to rambus memory
technology.  I have found an interesting presentation on this topic
by Bert McComas of InQuest Market Research available on the web at:

   http://www.platform99.com/images/PC133vsRmbs.pdf

Among the highlights of this report are:

 - rambus memory will cost about 70% more than PC100 SDRAM.

 - for a 256 MB desktop the extra cost of the RIMMs and all
   the bits and pieces to implement rambus is about $265.

 - current rambus demo systems fail without ECC enabled.

 - rambus ECC doesn't protect against transmission errors.

 - power management degrades latency, not bandwidth.

 - heat build up during benchmarks result in chipset throttling
   and reduced performance.

 - rumours that max channel loading needs to be cut in half to
   16 chips per channel for 800 Mbps operation (i.e. 128/256
   Mbyte max configurations with 64/128 Mb parts).

 - rambus repeaters will not work on 4 layer mother boards.

 - Intel is opposing dual standard (DIMM & RIMM) motherboards
   by OEMs out of fear of rambus transition not happening.

 - 1999 forecast for rambus parts has been shrinking from 250M
   to 170M down to as low as 30M (Intel forecasts 60M).

 - OEMs demand for PC100 memory growing in anticipation of
   for non-deployment of rambus.

 - Intel has an 815 north bridge for 133 MHz FSB and PC133
   memory; has project underway to certify BX chipset for
   133 MHz operation.

 - most DRAM vendors have scrapped their 64Mb rambus wafers
   and are delaying tape out of 128 Mb parts to concentrate
   on PC100 and PC133 SDRAM.

 - rambus interface on DRAM doesn't shrink with advanced
   process so area/cost adder increases in time for shrinks
   of a given generation part.

I cannot confirm nor do I endorse any of these claims but I
suggest anyone seriously interested in what Intel plans for
future PCs might want to have a look at Mr. McComas's present-
ation (although it is a large, ~800K PDF file).  There is also
a nice 3D drawing of the "rambus wind tunnel" cooling design.

If these reports of difficulties within the rambus camp are
true then I sure hope that Compaq has a plan B thought up for
the upcoming Alpha 21364 (EV7); it might arrive before its
memory does  ;-)
   

All opinions absolutely and strictly my own.
--
