Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> writes:

I hate to due this but...

>  - rambus memory will cost about 70% more than PC100 SDRAM.
> 
No one has any idea what DRDRAM will really cost.  My guess is
between 10-20%  The 70% figure is pure speculation.  We already pretty
much know what the chips will cost.  Any cost beyond that is just poor
design.


>  - rambus ECC doesn't protect against transmission errors.
> 
It would be humorous to see someone prove this.  The ECC does protect
against any and all failures that ECC on SDRAM does.

>  - power management degrades latency, not bandwidth.
> 
Power management always degrades latency, even on SDRAM.  The typical
power management techniques are to sleep devices(shut them down).
This is pretty standard.  

>  - rumours that max channel loading needs to be cut in half to
>    16 chips per channel for 800 Mbps operation (i.e. 128/256
>    Mbyte max configurations with 64/128 Mb parts).
> 
Rumors.  Well my rumor is that PC133 only give 133MB/s of bandwidth.
Rumors are easy to start, and sometimes true(PC133 x8 parts only give
133MB/s of bandwidth, really, do the math).




>  - rambus repeaters will not work on 4 layer mother boards.
> 
Repeaters, smeeters.  Repeater have always been a dead end, even with
SDRAM for the most part. 


>  - rambus interface on DRAM doesn't shrink with advanced
>    process so area/cost adder increases in time for shrinks
>    of a given generation part.
> 
Nor do any analog circuit for the most part.  And I doubt that PC266
uses only non-analog circuits.  


> If these reports of difficulties within the rambus camp are
> true then I sure hope that Compaq has a plan B thought up for
> the upcoming Alpha 21364 (EV7); it might arrive before its
> memory does  ;-)
>    
I can't comment on product deliver dates for either rambus or Alpha,
but I will say that the applications in the pc market place are
different than the applications on EV7.  I would also say that the
only design so far that actually reduces latency requires having the
memory controller on chip and currently DRDRAM is the only pin
effective option(since SLDRAM seems to have admitted that it died,
finally).

I still think the world is going to have to go to a packetized
interface whether I'm correct still remains to be seen. But with what I
see as the "right" direction in system design, we are going to have to
get away from using wider and wider busses.  Bandwidth per pin will
become important.  And an individual dram is simply a building block.
If people persist in quoting DRDRAM bandwidth per device, I only think
its fair that they do the same thing for SDRAM.  


Aaron Spink
not speaking for compaq



