On Wed, 24 Mar 1999 15:12:58 -0800, "rick@rick.rdperf.com"
<rick@rick.rdperf.com> wrote:

>P.S.  Those who have a bias against Craig haven't been clear about
>their entire connections either, but I guess it only matters when you
>are sticking up for someone.

I was System Manager of a small Cluster at the time of the original
"incident" and had been Diskeeper customer, completely satisfied with
the product.  My company also happened to have "Pharmaceutical" in
their name, though we did nothing more than data processing, and
Ciba-Geigy was not one of our clients.  And while you are correct
(from an earlier post) that the owner of a business is completely free
to let his conscience guide his decisions about who to do business
with and who not to...his actions scared me to death.

At the very least, we were at direct risk of having our support
similarly yanked if for some reason we were suspected of aiding and
abetting the evil Ritalin makers (sic).  It is also not that great a
leap of thinking to fear much worse possibilities: if a business owner
is so clearly willing to put his religious beliefs ahead of the
well-being of his business, couldn't he just as easily take it to the
next level, and ship updates of his product to "bad" companies that
would scramble their disks?  All's fair in the name of what's
"right"...right?  

No thank you- we ran directly to Raxco, purchased their product, and
removed Diskeeper from our system forever.  VMS was at the time
*heavily* used withing the pharmaceutical industry, and I have to
imagine we weren't alone in our response to his actions.

So yes, a business owner can follow his beliefs...but I think such a
business owner has no business attempting to sell a product to such a
wide cross-section of the public.  Maybe he should find a business
where the issue doesn't become such a big deal.  (It only magnifies
the problem that his beliefs are so non-mainstream...)

Duncan, who feels a customer similarly has every right to follow his
conscience when making a purchase, and will never get anywhere near
another ESI product as long as he lives.



