I have no idea what Unigraphics is, but if they're a halfway intelligent
company, they looked at the cost of engineering, marketing, selling, and
supporting their product on Alpha vs. the revenue. If that was not
attractive to them, especially if they gave it a couple years or so, then
they are right to bail on the port. Another possibility that commonly comes
up is that a company doesn't have the resources to continue a port. Either
becuase they're low on cash or because they cannot hire enough engineers to
do everything they want to do.

Note that many software companies expect something like a factor of 10
return on investment relative to engineering cost. Which is really hard to
achieve with a secondary focus port of another product. (Really depends on
accounting and engineering practices though. Ports can be really cheap or
really expensive depending on how its done.)

So much for basic business practices. As to marketing, they would have been
wiser to write a less confrontational end of life notice.

