Unigraphics is one of the top 2-3 high-end CAD/CAM/CAE packages out there,
for many years now.  Our main competitors would be CATIA, SDRC I-Deas, and
Pro/Engineer.  UG is in use at a LARGE number of sites out there, however,
the predominant platform until recently was HP-UX.  In the last few years,
along with the rest of the marketplace, UG introduced and has been gradually
improving a port of the system on Windows NT, both Alpha and Intel.

The Windows NT port was originally an almost direct X11 port, to
Hummingbird's Exceed X-11 package, enabling a large chunk of the code to be
portable between Alpha and Intel, as it was written on top of Exceed's XDK.
However, with version 16, Unigraphics will be rolling out (at the request of
many customers) a native Windows NT version.  I would have to guess that
this may have prompted the death of the Alpha NT port.

Unigraphics in past times has been a halfway intelligent company.  We occupy
the high-end, so I don't expect all Unix support will go away anytime soon. 
However (and much due to Digital's problems getting the Alpha platform
widely accepted), Digital UNIX has never been a strong platform, and many
companies that have adopted the Windows NT version are moving up to UG from
other PC-based CAD systems like AutoCAD.  Under that scenario, much of the
customers I've spoken with don't want to mix their platform space, so they
stick with what they now (i.e. Intel).  It's a shame that the Alpha port may
be dying, especially since I know of a 100-seat site that just bought a
bunch of the last run of 600au's, with the thought that if Digital UNIX
wasn't the way to go, they could always run NT...

Note that I'm still pursuing this internally, but all I've been able to
track down is a developer who thought that it wasn't likely to happen.  I
personally suspect that this is all tied in with the fact that UGS was a
part of the Pentium III Xeon launch (i.e. kill the Alpha and we'll help you 
tune your modeling kernel and put you into nationwide exposure during the 
PIII Xeon launch...).  I promise I'll followup with more detail as soon as I
see something official internally.

