In article <zalmanF8x1rC.2Hv@netcom.com>, Zalman Stern
<zalman@netcom.com> writes
>I have no idea what Unigraphics is, but if they're a halfway intelligent
>company, they looked at the cost of engineering, marketing, selling, and
>supporting their product on Alpha vs. the revenue.

I'll tell you a sad story (sad because I'm a DEC fan).  We sell a CAD
package which used to be fully supported on X86 (95, NT) and Alpha (NT).
Here are the main reasons we stopped supporting it on Alpha:

1. No port is cheap.  Even if there are no platform specific problems
(there were actually several such) you've added substantially to your
release time testing effort; and to the number of machines you need to
have available for testing & customer support.

2. Alpha was always many months behind Intel in the availability of up
to date versions of the C++ compiler and the MFC.  So we were stuck
living with out of date compilers.  Even then there were platform
specific compiler problems.

3. Alpha was always many months behind Intel in the availability of
versions of Office.  Even though this did not directly affect the code
we delivered, it meant that we either had to keep everyone back at the
old versions, or developers who primarily worked on Alpha had to have
access to an Intel in order to read Word/Excel documents.

4. We sold very, very few copies of the Alpha version.

The last one is, of course, the killer.  There's no point making a
product which no-one wants to buy, even if it does run on the fastest
CPU on the planet.
-- 
