Try this syntax:
>>>B/1 DUxx     (Invokes conversational boot)
SYSBOOT> SET START "MINI"    (sets startup to minimum quotes are required)
SYSBOOT > C    (continue command)

System will now boot and you can edit SYSTARTUP_V5.COM  (or your other specific
file ) to SET ON

Before you reboot you need to reset the minimum flag:

$ MCR SYSGEN    (invoke sysgen utility)
SYSGEN> SET START ""    (clears the mini flag)
SYSGEN > WRITE CURRENT    (writes flag to disk)
SYSGEN> ^Z    (exits)

Now reboot and you'll be all set.

MJB

SHass88010 wrote:

> >I have a Microvax II which will not complete booting because of a disk
> >failure.  The system disk is OK, and I get to the site specific boot
> >commands and it tries to mount the failed (user) disk and hangs waiting
> >for an operator to mount the requested volume.
> >
> >My question is, how can I bypass or interrupt the boot process at a
> >point where I can edit the startup file and comment out the mounting of
> >the failed disk.
> >
> >I have a backup of the volume, but my boot from TK-50 doesn't work
> >either, so I figure if I get the system up with the system disk, I can
> >restore the failed user disk.
> >
> >Thanks,
> >
> >Bill Fry
> >E-mail: william.h.fry@lmco.com
>
> You have to perform what is called a "conversational boot". This is initiated
> from the Chevron prompt (">>>"). You should find the specifics in your system
> manuals. You can pass a parameter to the boot process that prevents the system
> startup procedure from being run.  I do not remember the exact syntax, because
> its years since I have had to do this, but it is in the documentation. Once you
> boot the system via Conversational Boot, edit your system startup and put the
> command SET NOON at the beginning; this will prevent the procedure from exiting
> if it encounters any errors; it will just continue with the next command. Note
> that many system features will not be enabled, so you will probably have to use
> EDT in line mode, as distinct from screen mode, to edit your startup procedure.
> Once you have made your changes, just reboot and your system should come up OK.
> I have been in exactly the same situation and have got around it this way. I
> recommend always having a SET NOON in the startup procedure so that errors do
> not prevent the system from booting.
>
> Good luck,
> Sean




