In article <LXSdtBAikr92Ew4C@jjdash.demon.co.uk>,
John Aldridge  <jpsa@jjdash.demon.co.uk> wrote:
>3. Alpha was always many months behind Intel in the availability of
>versions of Office.  Even though this did not directly affect the code
>we delivered, it meant that we either had to keep everyone back at the
>old versions, or developers who primarily worked on Alpha had to have
>access to an Intel in order to read Word/Excel documents.

This is a digression from the point of your posting, but this paragraph
exemplifies an interesting paradox.

The received wisdom is that an architecture like Alpha couldn't supplant
IA-32 because the installed customer base would have to replace all of its
software. Yet Microsoft can change its Office suite every few years, and
corporate customers (like my employer) cheerfully pay to upgrade thousands
of licenses and install new copies of the program on thousands of machines,
because the old version can't read files written in the default format
generated by the new version, and as soon as somebody somewhere starts using
the new version, everybody else needs it. The economic "network effect"
that inhibits architectural change somehow promotes software change.

(Few people I work with could tell you what's in Office 97 that wasn't in
Office 95, and even fewer generate documents that require the new
version--except that by default the new version generates files which the old
version cannot read. Your company may well be different, of course. But I'll
cite a front-page Wall Street Journal article from a month or two ago as
evidence that many customers are underwhelmed by the "improvements".)

If Moore's law or fab costs ever hit the wall and processor speeds cease to
increase, will Intel respond by adding tailfins and racing stripes to its
architectures every few years just as Microsoft does to its Office suite,
deliberately introducing incompatibility to give customers an incentive
to upgrade?

-- 
Steven Correll == 1931 Palm Ave, San Mateo, CA 94403 == sjc@netcom.com



