> are fabbing their own kind, you are right, the Alpha would eat the
> Xeons for breakfast any day and continue to do so. But just one or
> two quarters of process slip and the advantage melts.
> 

Well, word has it that Intel was in no hurry to produce Alpha in 0.28u
CMOS7 at the Hudson Fab-6 plant formerly owned by DEC. Consequently, the
Q demanded that Intel produce beaucoup 0.35u Alpha wafers as per the
contractual agreement. Which caused supply constraints in Intel
StrongARM and other chips.

Intel finally saw the light and is now producing EV67 for the Q.

