Damon Love <gte822f@prism.gatech.edu> writes:

>ok, here's an update...  we have 2 solaris SPARC 2.6 Ultra 1 machines and
>4 LINUX Redhat 5.2 300Mhz AMD K6-2 machines mounting from the same
>DEC server...  all of them get around 2.5 Mbytes for NFS traffic...

>The slow down is really only from DEC to DEC machine (all 4.0D)...

Even Linux writing to DEC is good?  I've heard of problems due to
small wsize and lack of biod/nfsiod support.  The Linux man page
recommends mounting with rsize=8192,wsize=8192.  (Linux's default is 1024.)

>Also, when we are simply doing one file copy, we routinely get
>"NFS server not respsonding, still trying.  NFS server OK..."

That only happens if the client has to retransmit a request several times.
I'd start with looking at "netstat -i -s" data and would also time
streaming file transfers, e.g. copy a big file with ftp to /dev/null.
If the FTP data stream struggles in one direction or the other, there's
no point in looking at NFS, the problem will be with the network fabric.

>it's like it goes to sleep...  When I watch the server hard drive light when
>we are doing NFS tests it looks like it tramsits a little data waits,
>transmits
>a little more, waits...  These waits are maybe one second in duration!

Typical retransmit behaviour.

	-Ric Werme
