On Wed, 17 Mar 1999 16:30:20 -0500, jmd <jmd@infinet.com> wrote:

>Hi...
>I've had an Alpha Cabriolet with both Linux & NT for a while now.  In
>The Register I came across this reference:
>
>"Very soon now, Compaq will demonstrate Win64 on the Alpha platform. � "
>
>Is this a new NT? Can someone enlighten me to where I might find info?

"Win64" is, as Terry Shannon indicates, a nickname for some future
(probably several years out) version of Windows NT which has a full
64-bit API.  I have seen no details on how they plan to extend the
API, and wonder if they can do as good a job of it as OpenVMS Alpha
did.  OpenVMS Alpha had the advantage of argument list addresses
already being 64 bits, which made the process much simpler.

Windows 2000 (ready in 1901, as the joke goes), will contain an
extension for limited use of 64-bit addressable memory on Alpha
processors.  There are severe restrictions, such as you need to have
physical memory as the "backing store", only dynamic allocation
through new APIs is offered, allocations must be on large alignment
boundaries, and only a few API routines are available to deal with
64-bit address data.  The primary purpose of this is for database and
similar applications which want to maintain large in-memory
collections. 

In other words, it's nothing to get excited about yet...




