In article <7cq45k$2en$1@info.service.rug.nl>, helbig@man.ac.uk (Phillip Helbig) writes:
:Consider VT320 terminals and VAXstation 4000 and VAXstation 3100 
:machines.

..

:I'm looking for a concise description not of what wires are connected to
:what pins but rather of the differences in behaviour of various serial
:line connections. 

  I am aware of no such information -- each device, widget, plug,
  connector, etc., tends to be descrined separately.  (The old
  DECconnect product manuals may be the closest to what you want.)

: For instance, it seems that the `printer port' (not
:the `double arrow' port) DEC-423 socket has to be used for the console
:on a 3100,...

  Correct.

: and that the `double-arrow port' (not the `printer port') has
:to be used on the terminal.  

  VT-series terminals can (and must) have the communications ports 
  configured using the terminal setup displays.

:Basically, we have the two DEC-423 sockets
:on the terminal and an RS-232 connection.

  Yep.  Visit setup.  Global setup.  See the comm1 and communications
  sessions items in the global setup display.

:  On a 3100 we have two DEC-423
:sockets (labeled `printer' and `double arrow') but no RS-232 plug.  On a
:4000 we have one of each, each labeled with both. 

  Don't get confused over the connectors.  Rather, examine which jack
  you need to use (as in the case of the VAXstation 3100 console), or
  want to use (in the case of the VT420 terminal setup).

:We thus have 6 possible connections for the 3100 and 6 for the 4000.  Of 
:course, in the VT320 setup we can choose DEC-423 modem control, DEC-423 
:data leads only, RS-232 modem control, RS-232 data leads only.  Thus, we 
:could multiply the six connections by 4 to get 24 for each machine or 48 
:in all.

  Or four bazillion, if you add all the cabling permutations. :-)

:Of course, there are other terminals and other machines, but let's leave 
:it at that for now, except to throw in an ALPHAstation 255's two small 
:9-pin (PS/2 or what is it called) serial ports.

  There are two 9-pin connectors around -- the older VAX pinout, and
  the newer PS/2 pinout.  Adapters for both are listed in the OpenVMS
  FAQ.

:Any one care to detail (perhaps in condensed but complete and 
:understandable form) all possible connections and what they can/should 
:be used for?

  I am not aware of any complete list -- there are some good modem 
  writeups available over in Ask The Wizard.  If you want to pursue 
  this, get yourself a serial line analyzer -- often called a 
  "breakout box" -- and learn how to use it.

  There may be a book coming out with this information -- a chapter on 
  wiring and modems is planned.   Unfortunately, the schedule for the 
  availability of this chapter is still a while out. :-)  This stuff 
  is actually pretty easy, but is seldom described in an introductory 
  fashion.

:OK, that's terminals.  What connections should be used for terminal 
:servers, printers, non-console serial logins, modems etc.

  You need to look at the jack, at the plug, at the distance required,
  and the speeds likely involved, and match them all up.  Longer serial
  distances distances mean lower speeds, or require the use of differing 
  (typically balancing) signalling schemes.  See the EIA232 and EIA423
  standards for detailed information.

:Of course one can just try out all combinations, but as you can see 
:there are many, many of them and also perhaps the correct behaviour 
:depends on terminal set up parameters or even some setting within VMS.

  There are a few host terminal settings that can lead to corruptions 
  (eg: baud rate mismatches, flow control mismatches), though these are
  often easily recognized.

:Anyone familiar with the FALCO 5000 terminal?  I don't have mine here 
:with me at the moment, but is it possible that the RS-232 serial 
:connections on it are wired differently than on VT320s?  What is the 
:difference, if any, between the two serial connections on the FALCO?

  I hesitate to say the model of the last Falco I worked with, as
  that information would _really_ show my age. :-)

:Is there any reason not to use one terminal as the console for two 
:machines, switching in the terminal setup between RS-232 and DEC-423?

  Your proposed approach *usually* works.

  Beware: there are messages only displayed on the console (eg: pagefile
  warnings), and these can be lost when switching between systems.  Also, 
  if the terminal can (properly) send an XOFF, the OpenVMS host can 
  sometimes "jam up" when the console terminal stalls out.

:Life's too short for a serial line.

  I remember the fun of getting DECnet point-to-point to operate over 
  satellite links -- folks used to the more recently available and now
  more common terrestrial-based telephone links don't often appreciate 
  the delay involved in getting the packets up to geosynchronous orbit 
  and back down again... :-)  (But given the organization in your sig, 
  I suspect you have a more visceral understanding of this than most. :-)

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




