In article <7cp4t5$jbt@web.nmti.com>, Nick Manka <nick@baileynm.com> wrote:
>In article <7copna$8s8$1@panix7.panix.com>,
>	tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
>
>>>We've had similiar problems.
>>>
>>>A 3.2G machine could get about nine times the NFSv3 traffic to other
>>>machines as our 4.0D machine.
>> 
>> Are you *sure* both the switch and the machines running 4.0 agree on the
>> 10/100 and full/half duplex settings for the links to each machine?
>
>That was the first thing we checked :>

If possible, check it again.  Do the following test:

1) Set both sides to *autoselect* both 10/100 and duplex.

See how things work.

2) Set *the switch only* to hardwire it to 100MHz, full-duplex.

See how things work.

3) Set *the host only* to hardwire it to 100MHz, full-duplex.

See how things work.

4) Set both sides hardwired to 100MHz, full-duplex.

(I presume this is how it is now)

It's entirely possible -- I've seen it on other operating systems -- that the
DU 4.0 driver for the Tulip ethernet is botching the Nway negotiation when
it's hard-wired to 100Mb/sec mode or full-duplex mode or both.  Where I've
seen it before, generally option 1) or 2) above works best -- the key is to
make sure the host is set to autoselect.

-- 
