I doubt it.   Craig Jensen has, in my opinion, absolutely no
positive business ethics.

At the European DECUS symposium in Rome (September 1987), he
gave a presentation billed as an objective comparison of disk
defragmentation products.   It was simply a one-hour
commercial for his own product, knocking all the competition.

At the Spring 1988 DECUS symposium in Cincinnati, I posted
two notes in the NOTES conference about this incident.   There
was nothing beyond a simple statement of the facts, and my
opinion that I would not use his products because of this
violation of the DECUS canons.

He then threatened the VAX SIG with legal action unless they
deleted the notes. 

The SIG chair was scared enough that she immediately delete them.
She was in such a panic that, when she couldn't immediately
find John Covert (the NOTES moderator), she had his password
changed so she could log in to his account and do it herself.

According to notes posted on DECUServe, if you are engaged in
any activities that are objectional to scientologists, they
will not license the product to you.   If you somehow manage
to slip through, they will not renew your support contract
when they find out.

These are not the kind of people I want to let loose with
privileged code on my system.   Therefore, I make sure that
none of my clients ever use Executive Software products.

