> drives:
> 
> DKA300 A/3/0 Disk 1.05GB FX
> DKA400 A/3/0 Disk 1.05GB FX

This shows the problem. Both drives have the scsi id's set to '3' on the
'A' bus. They should have differnt id's.

What you need to do is get inside the computer case, and find the scsi
id jumpers, and set one of the drives to a different number, one which
is unused on the bus.

The only problems are:

1) identifying the correct jumper switches to play with
2) knowing how to set them

I'd recommend writing down the exact positions of the jumpers you find,
so you can allways get back to where you started. The jumpers may have
numbers on to help.

Trial and error may be ok, but the usual disclaimers apply!

In the end, from the >>> prompt, you'd see something like:

 DKA300 A/3/0 Disk 1.05GB FX
 DKA400 A/4/0 Disk 1.05GB FX

or whatever, but you'll have different id's, which is the main thing

I assume these are rz26's. I've never set/used one, so maybe others may
reply to you with setting info. It may be helpful if you tell us the
exact name/type of disks you have. this is written on a sticker on the
actual drive.

> 
> I can boot of either drive with the SET BOOT command without
> a problem but I can't then see the other drive from within
> VMS.
> 
> The MOUNT/FOREIGN command and the INITIALIZE command both
> fail from within VMS with the error NO SUCH DEVICE
> AVAILABLE.

Dont forget, initializing the disk will wipe whats on it....

