This seems to indicate packet loss for whatever reason (there are 
several). On a local network like yours you should see practically
zero drop rates. One way to check for this is to try:

ping -s 8000 -f client # On the server
ping -s 8000 -f server # On the client

In both cases, drop rates should be essentially zero (By the time I got
bored with this, I got less than 1 in 10,000 drop rate on our network, 
which is very similar to yours).

If you observe high drop rates, the first thing you want to check is
the full-duplex settings on your switch and DU box. If you use 
point-to-point links between switch and DEC client, full-duplex
should be either on or off on BOTH ends. If you have a hub between
the switch and the client, then you SHOULD NOT use full duplex on
any of the ports connected to the hub.

If this is Ok, check cabling (e.g. interchange the cables or sockets
used by one of the "good" clients and DEC box and see whether the
problem follows the cable or the computer). You can also get an
indication of cabling problems from netstat:

$ netstat -s -I tu0

tu0 Ethernet counters at Thu Mar 18 10:59:06 1999

       65535 seconds since last zeroed
  4294967263 bytes received
  4294967279 bytes sent
    53248824 data blocks received
   105348459 data blocks sent
   378081282 multicast bytes received
     3195790 multicast blocks received
      166038 multicast bytes sent
        2751 multicast blocks sent
           0 blocks sent, initially deferred
           0 blocks sent, single collision
           0 blocks sent, multiple collisions
vvvvvvvv   0 send failures
           0 collision detect check failure
           0 receive failures
           0 unrecognized frame destination
^^^^^^^^   0 data overruns
           0 system buffer unavailable
           0 user buffer unavailable

Large error counters between or on vvvvv/^^^^^ may indicate problems
with the cabling (or the ethernet card itself).

If this is Ok, check for possible reassembly problems on the client
due to low memory condition:

$ netstat -s -p ip
ip:
        111562229 total packets received
        0 bad header checksums
        0 with size smaller than minimum
        0 with data size < data length
        0 with header length < data size
        0 with data length < header length
        21706576 fragments received
---->   0 fragments dropped (dup or out of space)
---->   109 fragments dropped after timeout
        0 packets forwarded
        1021364 packets not forwardable
        512 packets denied access
        0 redirects sent
        0 packets with unknown or unsupported protocol
        92615577 packets consumed here
        90561036 total packets generated here
---->   1 lost packet due to resource problems
        3781800 total packets reassembled ok
        8073161 output packets fragmented ok
        47923501 output fragments created
        0 packets with special flags set

The lines marked with arrows should contain low numbers in
them. As a rule of thumb, if number of dropped fragments is 
within three orders of magnitude of the number of fragments 
received, you'd got a problem and shown tune network buffers.

Finally, bad ethernet controllers are not unheard of. If you
checked everything else, and are convinced that both the
cabling and the kernel network settings are fine, try borrowing
an identical ethernet card from a different system, and checking
whether it fixes the problem.

Regards,

/Serge.P



