Phillip Helbig (helbig@man.ac.uk) wrote:
> Consider VT320 terminals and VAXstation 4000 and VAXstation 3100 
> machines.

> I'm looking for a concise description not of what wires are connected to
> what pins but rather of the differences in behaviour of various serial
> line connections.  For instance, it seems that the `printer port' (not
> the `double arrow' port) DEC-423 socket has to be used for the console
> on a 3100, and that the `double-arrow port' (not the `printer port') has
> to be used on the terminal.  Basically, we have the two DEC-423 sockets
> on the terminal and an RS-232 connection.  On a 3100 we have two DEC-423
> sockets (labeled `printer' and `double arrow') but no RS-232 plug.  On a
> 4000 we have one of each, each labeled with both. 

On the VT320:

The double arrow connector is the DEC-423 connector that the VT uses to 
communicate with the host. Alternatively, use the 25-pin RS232 connector. 
Either will connect your VT to the host, as long as in set up you select 
the right connection - ie DEC-423 data leads only, or RS-232 data leads only.
(You're not using a modem, right?) 

The other modular connector on the VT320 is for you to attach a printer 
to. It is not used for attaching the terminal to the host.

On the VS3100, by flipping that little switch, you're telling the 
machine to use a device attached to the *PRINTER PORT* as the console, so 
you want to use the connector with the picture of a printer over it on 
the VAXstation.

I can't speak for the 4000 or Alphastation, though - I've not played with 
either.

> Anyone familiar with the FALCO 5000 terminal?  I don't have mine here 
> with me at the moment, but is it possible that the RS-232 serial 
> connections on it are wired differently than on VT320s?  What is the 
> difference, if any, between the two serial connections on the FALCO?

I have *no* idea, but a VT320 is wired as a DTE connection, same as the 
majority of other terminals, so it's probably the same. Check out the 
Richard S. Shuford's most splendid and complete web page at 
http://www.cs.utk.edu/~shuford/terminal_index.html for all things 
terminalish.

> Is there any reason not to use one terminal as the console for two 
