In article <7b7sis$1l0$1@netra.msu.montana.edu>,
 tarka@msu.oscs.montana.edu (Mark Tarka) writes:
|Suppose...the drive drew out a meter or two of tape while refusing
|to respond to the UNLOAD button on the TK-70 drive and the tape
|cartridge was finally removed by taking out the drive and releasing
|the lock by hand only to find...that the end of the tape was still
|in the drive.

Wear cotton gloves, and pull out the tape.  There's a round hole in
the tape (about the diameter of the heads of the four screws holding
the cartridge together) two feet from the end of the tape.  Then
comes the leader with its pick-up loop.  

|There's at least two interlocks on the cartridge (or so it seems).

Well, more than that.  On the door, near where it pivots, slide the
tab to the side to open the door (if is's not already held open by
the exposed tape).  Prop the door open (tweezers worked for me).

Looking at the cartridge with the open door at the top and the
bottom facing you, there is a rectangular opening near the lower
right-hand corner; a white plastic tab is visible inside -- a folded
twist-tie inserted into the hole will release the interlock.

The second interlock that has to be defeated is on the top surface,
where the door is, but on the left side.  Insert something into the
hole to toggle the interlock.

Glove one hand and guide the tape into the cartridge while rotating
the reel -- thumb pressure on the exposed drive-plate (the disk with
the 3/8 inch notches along the radii that engages with the drive
gear inside the tape-drive chassis) and rotation will rewind the
tape.  Position the pick-up loop by comparison to a good tape.

|Anyone with a clue as to how to manually rewind the tape back into
|the cartridge?

Well...don't attempt to take the cartridge apart.


There is one problem.  What is the correct position of the lead
inside the body of the tape-drive, the one that engages the pick-up
loop on the end of the tape?  Will the drive reset itself on
power-up, or will it be left in a permantly confused state?


  Mark






