In article <7ccdqd$4u2$1@news.ccit.arizona.edu>, Gordon C Zaft <zaft@u.arizona.edu> writes:
:Robert Young <rcyoung@groupz.net> wrote:
:>  am in the process of planning an upgrade for VMS from 6.1 to 6.2 (and
:> then to 7.1). The idea is to have a develpment system that can dual boot
:> (2) different versions of the OS.

  A system cannot have two versions on the same system disk without someone 
  first figuring out some (unsupported) way of dealing with the overlapping
  use of the VMS$COMMON directory structure by both OpenVMS versions.


:> The manual refers to booting from [SYSF]. I do not have all  the manuals
:> specific for this model Vax. Does anyone know the proper command???


  http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/ (yes, port 81 also still works) 
  has the OpenVMS documentation.


:You would have to install the new version onto an alternate root, which could
:be SYSF or any other number not in use (0 - F, 0 is the default root).

  On OpenVMS VAX, SYSF is reserved for use by OpenVMS upgrades.  SYSE is
  reserved for standalone BACKUP.  SYS0 through SYSD are available.

:	Once you have that you can boot the alternate root by doing a
:b /r5=F I think.

  On recent VAX systems including the entire VAX 4000 series:
    >>> b/r5:f0000000

  On older VAX systems:
    >>> b/f0000000

  On the oldest VAX systems:
    see the EXCHANGE utility, create a "SYFBOO.CMD" altering the deposit 
    in R5 in DEFBOO.CMD.  (Details are included in the processor-specific 
    portions of the documentation; in the OpenVMS VAX Upgrade and 
    Installation manual.)

  Booting from SYSF -- since that root is specifically part of the 
  OpenVMS upgrade -- is covered in detail in appendix C of the V7.2 
  OpenVMS VAX Upgrade and Installation Manual.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




