Damon Love (damon.love@gtri.gatech.edu) wrote:
: we have 10 DEC Alpha 400Mhz 164 machines running DC UNIX 4.0D.
: we are running all of them on a 100Mbit Ethernet connected with a 3COM
: switch.

: we are getting pitiful NFS performance... at best 400kb per second!

Well, something is badly wrong with your configuration and/or hardware.
On an almost identical setup (DPW 500au, 3COM 3300 in full-duplex mode)
we get close to 3Mbytes/second for NFS writes (that's NFS over UDP, 
default mount options) and little bit under 8Mbytes/second for NFS
reads. (Both numbers are for sequential reads and writes with large 
block size from/to a 200Mbyte file. Buffer caches on either system were
under 5Mbytes, and shouldn't have distorted the results too much).
I suspect that the latter number is in fact limited by the throughtput 
of the 7200RPM drives we are using and not by NFS as such.

Check error counters on the ethernet interfaces and switch - bad NFS
performance may indicate high drop rates on your LAN, which in turn
may be caused by inappropriate settings on switch ports (like setting
full-duplex mode on a wire going to a hub) or low-quality cabling.

Regards,

/Serge.P



