>
>We are considering integrating a NT file server with NFS to support a
>variety of UNIX (OSF 4.0D) based ALPHA workstations. Products we have
>come across include Onnet32, DiskShare, BW-COnnect and Netmanage.

  I have to say that the advice of Thor Simon is really good.
Solaris x86 is stable, fast, and will cost less than the NT based 
package.  

  I have to ask, though -- why get a PC at all?  If you already have
alpha based systems running OSF, why not just put disks on one of those
machines and make that machine the NFS server?  (I can think of several
good reasons, but none of them seem compelling).  After all, it is better
to have fewer boxes than more, and better to have all identical boxes
than many different boxes.  If you need NT type services, you can always
use a unix package to provide them (samba comes to mind, but I think
there are commercial packages from sun and AT&T).  A PC that is a
reasonable server will cost almost as much as a workstation, and chances
are the "workstation" already uses plenty of PC commodity parts
(almost all PCI workstations can identify and use symbios 8xx scsi cards,
most can use at least one kind of ethernet card).
chris



