> variety of UNIX (OSF 4.0D) based ALPHA workstations. Products we have
> come across include Onnet32, DiskShare, BW-COnnect and Netmanage.
> 
> Our motiviation is to reduce costs by going to commodity (intel) chip.
> 

If you want to run an Intel box as an NFS fileserver 
to save costs, why not run UNIX on that server instead 
of NT. 

There are a number of commecial UNIX's for Intel, Sun 
Solaris for x86. There are also a number of Free UNIX's 
such as FreeBASD and Linux which also support NFS. 

If you are looking for NFS fileserver performance then 
Solaris is probably quickest though this is more of 
a guess based on SPECSFS results for Solaris on SPARC. 

All of them will be quicker and more robust than using NT.
They all have the advantage of supporting the same Name Server 
e.g NIS/NIS+ and a simular set of management interfaces.

> Info from anyone who is doing this would be greatly appreciated,
> particularly as regard to reliability, speed, etc. as compared to a UNIX
> based file server.
> 
> Would also be interested in anybody who has had to deal with the "big
> endian"/"little endian" problem when trying to share files between HP/UX
> system and NT box.
> 

This should not be a problem. If you use NFS, then data is put into 
common format called XDR (External Data Representation) when it is 
put out over the wire. 


