From: patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii)
Subject: Re: Help: Digital Unix vx Linux
Date: 1999/03/10
Message-ID: <7c4g8u$gui@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 453200210
References: <Pine.A41.4.05.9903091252120.47672-100000@homer24.u.washington.edu> <36E5AFBB.D38A1DDD@rhea.umd.edu>
Organization: The University of Calgary
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Jeffrey J. Potoff (jpotoff@rhea.umd.edu) wrote:
: > I have digital 500au system running Digital Unix 4.0D.
: > I am thinking to replace Unix with Linux, because Linux has alot of
: > freeware to download.

: And so does Digital Unix.  What exactly runs under Linux that won't run
: under DU ?

Quite a lot of free software will in fact run on Linux but not on DU, 
either due to the fact that it relies on buglets in Linux, or due
to genuinely missing features. For example xcdroast (a simple, but 
lovely CD mastering GUI) does not run on DU, and the port is not
quite trivial due to the dependence on the generic SCSI interface
and loopback device mounts in Linux.

Other Linux features missing in DU which may be important for personal
use include: em86, which allows to run many Linux/intel binaries (including
commercial software not available on Linux/alpha) on Linux/alpha; support
for Joliet CDs, NTFS and LAN Manager file systems; very extensive support
of PC peripherals not supported on DU (like inexpensive EIDE hard disks
and parallel-attached devices); somewhat smaller memory footprint; and 
probably many others.

Even in cases where a software package is supported on both Linux and
DU, the probabilility of finding a binary distribution is much higher
for Linux - and this is quite important for some (in fact most) people.

DU has its advantages, but these are not immediately apparent (and mostly
irrelevant) for a personal system.

Regards,

/Serge.P



