  The text or the transcription is wrong.

  When working on a VAXstation, having an external serial console can
  be handy -- OPCOMs can be routed there, as are xdelta breakpoints 
  and other such oddities, and there's somewhere to log in and restart
  when I do something that detonates DECwindows.  If you have used the
  graphics console on a workstation and particularly with OPA0: enabled
  as an operator, you should understand why... (I have reconfigured most
  every VAXstation or AlphaStation I have had to deal with to use an 
  alternate console.)

  You can switch from the VAXstation graphics console over to a serial 
  console using a switch, specically a switch usually labeled S3.  It 
  is on the back of the VAXstation 3100 series, though hidden behind 
  some plastic on the very earliest of the models and requiring the 
  top be removed -- with all later 3100 systems having the access hole.
  S3 is behind the front door on the VAXstation 4000 series.  There was 
  a weird cable used on the VAXstation 2000 -- the BCC08 -- that had
  shorted pins 8 and 9 to tell the system to use the printer port as 
  the system console.


:|> If you remove the keyboard, your VAXstation will start up as a microVAX.

  What happens when you remove the workstation keyboard varies, typically 
  it switches from a VAXstation to a VAXserver as far as licensing is 
  concerned.    

