There's also a mini-debugger in there, on at least some machines, from which
it's theoretically possible to recover from catastrophic mistakes like blowing
away the next stage -- like, say, with the hideously buggy firmware loader
for the AlphaPC 64, which does that about 50% of the time... :-/

It can be problematic to make it "go", though, because its autobauding often
only works at whatever (low) processor clock the board in question was
originally tested with, and finding an appropriate oscillator to run a
machine that shipped with a processor 2X or 3X as fast at the "right" system
clock can be tough -- DEC tended to use fairly pricy and obscure parts for
this.

