In article <36DF3668.829CDC8B@worldnet.att.net>, OPCOM <vaxhackery@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
>Pentiae, if it be latin..?

No.  Definitely not.  -ae as plural nominative is only for 1st 
Declension (usually feminine) where singular normally ends in -a.

-um nominative ending is most often 2nd Declension, neuter.  Neuter 
plurals are always -a., Hence "Pentia" is fairly probable if "Pentium" 
were a Latin word - which it isn't.

>Sound the Klaxon! Boot the VAXen!
>
>Mitchell Nussbaum wrote:
>> 
>> Gary Heston wrote in message <7bfhri$de2$1@sh1.ro.com>...
>> >In article <7b9kiu$9ps$6@netnews.upenn.edu>,
>> >Paul Hughett <hughett@chaplin.med.upenn.edu> wrote:
>> >  [ ... ]
>> >>By the way, what's the plural of Pentium?     [ ... ]
>> 
>> Wouldn't it be "Pentii"?

-i ending for nominative plural is only for 2nd Declension Masculine, 
where the nominative singular ending is typically -us. As in "Pentius".
Penti - us?  No, not us.  We wouldn't do a thing like that!

>> >"Pendingum", as in "the release is pending, um, somtime....".

Precisely!



