In article <36DBF910.F3FC8C67@Sun.COM>,
Russell Crook  <russell.crook@Sun.COM> wrote:

>I've often wondered why heat pipe based heat sinks have
>never caught on, but more expensive/complex solutions (such as
>Kryotron's (sp?) Peltier chillers) have ... any ideas anyone?
>Mechanical room for the heat pipe and necessary airflow, perhaps?

A heat pipe has a hot end at temperature T1, and a cold end at
temperature T2.  Heat transfer is highest if T1 allows the working
fluid to change phase (preferably boil), and if T2 allows vapor to
condense.

Quite often, the heat pipe designer has trouble finding a cheap,
non-corrosive working fluid that suits the T1 and T2 imposed on him.

Also, seals are often a problem, although I'm not sure why. One can
change the boiling point of a fluid by changing its pressure, and that
might be connected to the seals problems.

Of course, most heat problems are easily solved by just running some
plumbing through the chassis, and pumping chilled water around. Such
designs have expensive installation, plus there's an external unit,
plus there is mechanical maintenance, plus nuisance whenever cards
go in or out.  The mainframe experience was that "air cooled"
mainframes were quite a lot cheaper to own.

The IBM 370/168 was water cooled. It's been claimed that when they
were retired, every 168 chassis had rust from some little backflushing
accident during scheduled maintenance.
