lindsay@best.com (Donald C. Lindsay) writes:
>Of course, most heat problems are easily solved by just running some
>plumbing through the chassis, and pumping chilled water around. Such
>designs have expensive installation, plus there's an external unit,
>plus there is mechanical maintenance, plus nuisance whenever cards
>go in or out.  The mainframe experience was that "air cooled"
>mainframes were quite a lot cheaper to own.

This reminds me of two water cooled machines I worked with:
Elbrus-B and Elbrus-3. The -B (**) had chips glued by traditional "milk"
glue to frames, these frames were sliding into chassis with plumbing.
The -3 had  higher integration in better ECL chips so frame
was not enough. Instead a "sousage" filled with a liquid metal (Tallium, if
I remember well) was pressed against a row of chips. The circulating
metal removed the heat to the frame, frame passed heat to water.

(**) Elbrus-B was not a Prof. Babayan design, but a BESM-6 successor.

--Pete



