Performance and stability will suck.  You'll also probably have problems
because NT's file permissions don't map 1:1 to the UNIX ones.

Fact is, UNIX makes a much better file server for NT machines (using Samba)
than NT does for UNIX machines.  Which is about what I'd expect.

Unfortunately, neither Linux nor *BSD is really a tremendously good NFS
server.  They both work, and they'll both work better than some awful
hacked-together NFS server on NT, but that's not saying much.  Linux is
particularly bad as an NFS server, because the NFS server runs as a
user process and all the data has to be copied across the user/kernel 
divide.  BSD does better, but it's not as good as Solaris, which isn't
surprising; Sun invented NFS, and their NFS server is *really good*.

Just pick up a copy of Solaris/x86 -- it'll cost you about what NT
Server would have, if not less -- and install it on the same hardware
you planned to install NT on.  You'll be very pleased with the result.

NFS performance will be even better if you install Solstice DiskSuite
and enable metadata logging for the partition you're NFS-serving,
but that's a bit complicated and you can always do it later.

