I don't know about that one, but ... see

http://www.research.digital.com/wrl/techreports/abstracts/92.1.html

for a treatise on packaging a potential 150W ECL processor
(back when people thought ECL was the only way to consume
that much power in a single package :->)

A good chunk of the discussion is about a thermosiphon
(or "wickless heatpipe") based heat sink. (One minor disadvantage of
the thermosiphon is that it had to remain vertical, but it was thought
it would be cheaper to mass produce than a wick-based
heat pipe. A mercury tip switch was included
to cut off chip power if the system was tipped over.)
Significant air flow was still required, however.

I've often wondered why heat pipe based heat sinks have
never caught on, but more expensive/complex solutions (such as
Kryotron's (sp?) Peltier chillers) have ... any ideas anyone?
Mechanical room for the heat pipe and necessary airflow, perhaps?
