: There were so many packets that the network (10MB ethernet) was *totally*
: flodded -- the backbone switch was lit up like a disco with all the 
: collisions! I have seen this effect with 4.0d and 4.0e -- the 4.0d
: client did not last long and was running linux after one week of this
: behaviour. Now I just got a couple of XP1000 boxes and the problem
: reappeared after the first day! If anyone has any thoughts on this please
: post and/or email me.

Yes, we'd seen this behavior between 4.0d clients and 4.0d servers.
It seems to be related to a rapid succession of unlink() and rename()
to the unlink()'ed name involving a modified mmap()'ed file on an
NFS file system mounted hard,nintr. After that point, the client 
floods the server with invalid remove NFS V3 requests. Since these 
requests originate in the client's kernel, reboot is the only remedy. 

This happened consistently when one of our users was compiling a 
small Fortran source in an NFS-mounted directory.

Regards,

/Serge.P



