In article <zalmanF7wv8A.AzK@netcom.com> zalman@netcom.com (Zalman Stern) writes:
>Chris Morgan (mihalis@ix.netcom.com) wrote:
>: My copy of the William Stallings book analyses both the VAX and the
>: 360 as examples at (or near) two ends of the spectrum and has them
>: coming out about even in terms of performance.
>
>What spectrum is this?
>
>: Not to argue, but could you explain where you differ?
>
>Haven't read the reference so I can't say precisely where I differ. My
>argument is that the 360 instruction format is easier to pipeline than the
>VAX. The 360 has fewer instruction lengths and you know the instruction
>length early on in the encoding. (Typically from the opcode IIRC.) The hard
>stuff in the 360 tends to be more obscure, like COBOL edit field handling
>and character translation. On the VAX, the hair starts with stuff like
>procedure calls. This is important 'cause you can relegate COBOL edit
>instructions to slow microcode without undue pain in many scenarios. But
>you have to make procedure calls go fast no matter what.
>
>: I think x86 is
>: basically in the weeds here as there is some weasel phrase to do with
>: all things being equal, process technology wise. If all things were
>: equal Intel would not be the behemoth it is.
>
>Part of the trick of success is making sure things aren't equal. Once
>again, I claim the x86 is easier because the instruction set has fewer
>impediments to pipelining. It used to be thought that it was impossible to
>heavily pipeline a VAX. Which is not true, but it took a lot of effort.
>(Any architectural problem can be solved given enough gates.)
>
>On the flipside, with modern microarchitectures, these issues probably just
>comes down to having a larger prefetch window at the front of an OOO
>pipeline anyway.
>
>-Z-


-- 




