>All of our documentation belongs to the customer and so is not in the
>building, the closest I have come is that I now know that the TCP/IP stuff
>is managed by a program called ucx, and that a VAX VMS Training manual
dated
>November 1984 does not have the Information I need.


Try and obtain the VMS online documntation set CDs. They are dished out free
every 3 months if you are on a software support contract.

You also need a machine capable of running X-window, then fire up the
"bookreader" application on the VMS machine. By the way, the product UCX is
now referred to as "TCP-IP Services for OpenVMS" almost exclusively in the
documentation.

In terms of specific help about UCX, the permanent configuration stuff (i.e.
that which survives a reboot) is done by @ucx$config. This is all menu
driven and quite simple to manage.

Ivor.






