for a long time self-modifying instructions (i.e. the currently
executing instruction could modify anything in the next instruction
... including the opcode) made pipelining hard ... especially on the
lower-end microcoded 360/370 machines. some simple tests in the 70s
would show speedups of 3-4 times in thruput if self-modifying was
deactivated. 

i confess to also being guilty of doing it once ... for a test
instruction in one of the page replacement implementations that i did
for the operating system.

