In article <36d7a8e4.770725@nntp.source.co.uk>, duncan@ironhorse.source.co.uk (Duncan Entwisle) writes:

  OpenVMS questions are best handled in comp.os.vms.

:I've followed the instructions on how to set a new password for SYSTEM
:as described in the FAQ, however I'm having problems.
..
:I've gotten past the SYSBOOT> prompt, to the dollar prompt. I type
:SYS$SYSTEM:STARTUP, whereupon it displays a message about ignoring the
:label (SYS$SYSTEM - I'm *very* new to VMS, but I thought this was a
:path - or is label VMS terminalogy for a path?), and the startup
:command prompts me with "What?".


  You apparently forgot the "@" character listed in step 4.  From the
  OpenVMS FAQ:

>  ...
>
>  4.  At the $ prompt, the system will now be accepting startup commands
>      directly from the console.  Type the following two DCL commands:
>
>        SPAWN
>        @SYS$SYSTEM:STARTUP
>
>      The result of these two commands will be the normal system startup,
>      but you will be left logged in on the console, running under a
>      privileged username.  Without the use of the SPAWN command, you
>      would be logged out when the startup completes.
>
>  ...

:Its obviously expecting a parameter. I've tried DUA0:, MICROVAX (the
:label for DUA0: as reported by SHOW DEVICES) etc, but its says device
:not in configuration file, or not online.

  The colon is what is causing the message you are seeing -- you are 
  entering what looks like a label followed by an invalid command.

  The lack of the "@" prefix on the file specification is why the 
  specified command procedure is not being invoked.

  I would strongly recommend availing yourself of the available
  OpenVMS documentation:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com:81/

  This is probably newer than the version of OpenVMS in use, but it
  will give you a good basis.  The two manuals to start with are the
  user's guide and the system manager's manual...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
